  <section xmlns='http://docbook.org/ns/docbook' xml:id='category-OPERATIONS'>
    <title>Operations</title>
    <para>An operation is a sequence of things: values, names, operator names, tuples in parentheses, vector literals in brackets and blocks in braces, each perhaps followed by a member access with a dot. The compiler reads the sequence from left to right and holds one current value; the TYPE of that value says what the next thing means. A tuple after a function is the call, a tuple after a type the construction, a vector literal after a value the indexing, an operator name after a value one of the operators its type declares. Parentheses, brackets, braces and the dot are what structure a sequence, and nothing else does. An operation ends with a colon, a line break, or anything that is not part of it.</para>

    <section xml:id='tests-PREFIX-FUNCTIONS'>
      <title>Prefix Operators</title>
      <para>Where a value is expected an operator name is a prefix: it takes the next value as its operand. A value is expected at the start of an operation, after an operator, after a function, and after another prefix, so <literal>a - -2</literal> subtracts minus two, <literal>twice -5</literal> is <literal>twice(-5)</literal>, and <literal>not not a</literal> reads as written. An <literal>=</literal> at the start of an operation does nothing, so <literal>let a = 42</literal> reads as <literal>let a 42</literal>.</para>
