    <section xml:id='tests-OPERATOR-CHAINS'>
      <title>Operator Chains</title>
      <para>Several operators in a row combine by their binding strength, which the standard library states as a protocol of pending types: the compiler itself has no precedence table. Strongest first, the levels are <literal>*</literal> <literal>/</literal> <literal>%</literal> <literal>div</literal> <literal>mod</literal>; <literal>+</literal> <literal>-</literal>; <literal>&lt;&lt;</literal> <literal>&gt;&gt;</literal>; <literal>&lt;</literal> <literal>&gt;</literal> <literal>&lt;=</literal> <literal>&gt;=</literal>; <literal>=</literal> <literal>&lt;&gt;</literal>; <literal>&amp;</literal>; <literal>xor</literal>; <literal>|</literal>; <literal>and</literal> <literal>&amp;&amp;</literal>; <literal>or</literal> <literal>||</literal>. An operator a type declares that no level names, such as <literal>==</literal> or a text name, binds most weakly of all. Operators of one level combine from left to right.</para>
