              <entry><literal>not true</literal></entry>
              <entry><literal>false
        </literal></entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry><literal>not false</literal></entry>
              <entry><literal>true
        </literal></entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry><literal>~true</literal></entry>
              <entry><literal>false
        </literal></entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry><literal>~false</literal></entry>
              <entry><literal>true

    </literal></entry>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </tgroup>
      </informaltable>
    </section>

    <section xml:id='tests-BOOLEAN-AND-OR'>
      <title>Boolean And/Or</title>
      <para>The operators <literal>and</literal> and <literal>or</literal> (also spelled <literal>&amp;&amp;</literal> and <literal>||</literal>) are declared by the standard library with a lazy right operand: the right side is evaluated only where the left one does not decide. The bitwise operators <literal>&amp;</literal>, <literal>|</literal> and <literal>xor</literal> combine two bools as well, and evaluate both sides.</para>
      <informaltable frame='none'>
        <tgroup cols='2'>
          <colspec colwidth='1*'/>
          <colspec colwidth='1*'/>
          <thead>
            <row>
              <entry>Input</entry>
              <entry>Result</entry>
            </row>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <entry><literal>true and false</literal></entry>
              <entry><literal>false
        </literal></entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry><literal>false or true</literal></entry>
              <entry><literal>true
        </literal></entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry><programlisting>procedure noisy() returns bool
{
    print "evaluated"
    true
}
false and noisy()</programlisting></entry>
              <entry><literal>false
        </literal></entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry><programlisting>procedure noisy() returns bool
{
    print "evaluated"
    false
}
true or noisy()</programlisting></entry>
              <entry><literal>true
        </literal></entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry><literal>true &amp; true</literal></entry>
              <entry><literal>true
        </literal></entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry><literal>true &amp; false</literal></entry>
              <entry><literal>false
        </literal></entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry><literal>false | false</literal></entry>
              <entry><literal>false
        </literal></entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry><literal>true | false</literal></entry>
              <entry><literal>true
        </literal></entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry><literal>true xor true</literal></entry>
              <entry><literal>false
</literal></entry>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </tgroup>
      </informaltable>
    </section>
  </section>

  <section xmlns='http://docbook.org/ns/docbook' xml:id='category-BITWISE-OPERATORS'>
    <title>Bitwise Operators</title>
    <para>Bitwise operators manipulate integer values at the bit level.</para>

    <section xml:id='tests-BIT-SHIFTS'>
      <title>Bit Shifts</title>
      <para>The left shift operator shifts bits to the left, effectively multiplying by powers of two. The right shift operator shifts bits to the right, effectively dividing by powers of two.</para>
      <informaltable frame='none'>
        <tgroup cols='2'>
          <colspec colwidth='1*'/>
          <colspec colwidth='1*'/>
          <thead>
            <row>
              <entry>Input</entry>
              <entry>Result</entry>
            </row>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <entry><literal>1 &lt;&lt; 4</literal></entry>
              <entry><literal>16
        </literal></entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry><literal>5 &lt;&lt; 3</literal></entry>
              <entry><literal>40
        </literal></entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry><literal>16 &gt;&gt; 2</literal></entry>
              <entry><literal>4
        </literal></entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry><literal>100 &gt;&gt; 3</literal></entry>
              <entry><literal>12
</literal></entry>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </tgroup>
      </informaltable>
    </section>
  </section>

  <section xmlns='http://docbook.org/ns/docbook' xml:id='category-LAMBDAS'>
    <title>Lambdas</title>
    <para>A lambda is an anonymous function written with a backslash:
    <literal>\params: body</literal>. Parameters are space-separated. A lambda bound to a name
    and applied to arguments is monomorphized at the call site for those
    argument types, so the same lambda works at any argument type.</para>

    <section xml:id='tests-LAMBDA-APPLICATION'>
      <title>Lambda Application</title>
      <para>A lambda bound to a name can be called like a function.</para>
      <informaltable frame='none'>
        <tgroup cols='2'>
          <colspec colwidth='1*'/>
          <colspec colwidth='1*'/>
          <thead>
            <row>
              <entry>Input</entry>
              <entry>Result</entry>
            </row>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <entry><programlisting>let inc \x: x + 1
inc(5)</programlisting></entry>
              <entry><literal>6
        </literal></entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry><programlisting>let add \x y: x + y
add(3, 4)</programlisting></entry>
              <entry><literal>7
        </literal></entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry><programlisting>let dbl \x: x + x
let a dbl(10)
let b dbl(21)
a + b</programlisting></entry>
              <entry><literal>62

    </literal></entry>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </tgroup>
      </informaltable>
    </section>

    <section xml:id='tests-LAMBDA-CAPTURES'>
      <title>Captures</title>
      <para>A lambda captures enclosing variables by value. The captured
        value is fixed when the lambda is applied.</para>
      <informaltable frame='none'>
        <tgroup cols='2'>
          <colspec colwidth='1*'/>
          <colspec colwidth='1*'/>
          <thead>
            <row>
              <entry>Input</entry>
              <entry>Result</entry>
            </row>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <entry><programlisting>let n 10
let f \x: x + n
f(5)</programlisting></entry>
              <entry><literal>15
        </literal></entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry><programlisting>let a 3
let b 4
let g \x: x + a + b
g(10)</programlisting></entry>
              <entry><literal>17

    </literal></entry>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </tgroup>
      </informaltable>
    </section>

    <section xml:id='tests-LAMBDA-BLOCK-CAPTURES'>
      <title>Captures in Braced Bodies</title>
      <para>Capture discovery reaches inside a braced body, and a body-local
        binding that shadows an enclosing name is the local, not a capture.</para>
      <informaltable frame='none'>
        <tgroup cols='2'>
          <colspec colwidth='1*'/>
          <colspec colwidth='1*'/>
          <thead>
            <row>
              <entry>Input</entry>
              <entry>Result</entry>
            </row>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <entry><programlisting>let n 10
let cap \x: { x + n }
cap(5)</programlisting></entry>
              <entry><literal>15
        </literal></entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry><programlisting>let n 10
let shade \x: { let n 99
x + n }
shade(5)</programlisting></entry>
              <entry><literal>104

    </literal></entry>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </tgroup>
      </informaltable>
    </section>

    <section xml:id='tests-LAMBDA-EMBEDDED'>
      <title>Embedded Calls</title>
      <para>A lambda call may appear anywhere inside a larger expression, not
        only as the whole right-hand side of a binding. Like every application,
        each call takes its arguments before the operators around it combine.</para>
      <informaltable frame='none'>
        <tgroup cols='2'>
          <colspec colwidth='1*'/>
          <colspec colwidth='1*'/>
          <thead>
            <row>
              <entry>Input</entry>
              <entry>Result</entry>
            </row>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <entry><programlisting>let inc \x: x + 1
let add \x y: x + y
inc(5) + add(3, 4)</programlisting></entry>
              <entry><literal>13
        </literal></entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry><programlisting>let inc \x: x + 1
let n 100
n + inc(9)</programlisting></entry>
              <entry><literal>110
        </literal></entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry><programlisting>let n 100
let cap \x: x + n
cap(1) + cap(2)</programlisting></entry>
              <entry><literal>203

    </literal></entry>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </tgroup>
      </informaltable>
    </section>

    <section xml:id='tests-LAMBDA-DELEGATES'>
      <title>Delegate Values</title>
      <para>A <literal>delegate</literal> names a function type. A non-capturing lambda assigned
        to a delegate-typed binding is a function-pointer value that can be passed
        to a function and called indirectly. A lambda literal may also be passed
        straight to a delegate parameter, and so may a named function.</para>
      <informaltable frame='none'>
        <tgroup cols='2'>
          <colspec colwidth='1*'/>
          <colspec colwidth='1*'/>
          <thead>
            <row>
              <entry>Input</entry>
              <entry>Result</entry>
            </row>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <entry><programlisting>define IntFn delegate(int) returns int
function apply(f: IntFn, x: int) returns int
    f(x)
let g: IntFn \x: x + 1
apply(g, 5)</programlisting></entry>
              <entry><literal>6
        </literal></entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry><programlisting>define IntFn delegate(int) returns int
function twice(f: IntFn, x: int) returns int
    f(f(x))
let dbl: IntFn \x: x + x
twice(dbl, 20)</programlisting></entry>
              <entry><literal>80

    </literal></entry>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </tgroup>
      </informaltable>
    </section>

    <section xml:id='tests-LAMBDA-CLOSURES'>
      <title>Closures</title>
      <para>A capturing lambda assigned to a delegate type is a true closure
        value carrying the captured variables. (Closures returned from a
        function escape onto the caller's region -- see the escaping-closure AOT
        regression test.)</para>
      <informaltable frame='none'>
        <tgroup cols='2'>
          <colspec colwidth='1*'/>
          <colspec colwidth='1*'/>
          <thead>
            <row>
              <entry>Input</entry>
              <entry>Result</entry>
            </row>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <entry><programlisting>define IntFn delegate(int) returns int
function apply(f: IntFn, x: int) returns int
    f(x)
let n 10
let add: IntFn \x: x + n
apply(add, 5)</programlisting></entry>
              <entry><literal>15
</literal></entry>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </tgroup>
      </informaltable>
    </section>
  </section>
